From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] server, ntdll, wow64: job limits kept, read back and held

A job kept only its limit flags, and read even those back as zeros;
JobObjectBasicUIRestrictions was a stub (in 64-bit and WoW64); nothing held
the active process limit or the priority class and affinity limits.
Chromium's sandbox puts its renderers and utilities in jobs limited to one
active process, with UI restrictions; other programs read their job's
limits back.

- The server keeps a job's limit values (struct job_limits: user time
  limits, working set, affinity, process and job memory, active process
  limit, priority and scheduling class) and its UI restrictions; new
  requests set_job_ui_restrictions and get_job_limits (protocol change).
- NtQueryInformationJobObject answers JobObjectBasicLimitInformation,
  JobObjectExtendedLimitInformation and JobObjectBasicUIRestrictions with
  what was set; NtSetInformationJobObject checks the priority and
  scheduling classes and the UI restriction bits.
- The active process limit holds: a process is not assigned to a job (or
  one nested above it) at its limit, and a process in such a job cannot
  start another (STATUS_QUOTA_EXCEEDED, ERROR_NOT_ENOUGH_QUOTA); the job's
  completion port hears JOB_OBJECT_MSG_ACTIVE_PROCESS_LIMIT.
- A job's priority class and affinity limits apply to its processes, when
  they join and when the limits are set.
- winnt.h: the JOB_OBJECT_UILIMIT_* values.

Wine's kernel32 process tests: a test that the limit flags read back is
added (it fails without this); no failures.

Gate: test/joblimits-gate.sh (64- and 32-bit). Mutants
SG_MUTANT_NO_JOB_PROCESS_LIMIT (server/process.c),
SG_MUTANT_NO_JOB_LIMIT_VALUES (ntdll/unix/sync.c).
